2026-06-30 15:51:11,074 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-aa80cc35-edbd-4ab6-a100-78f9431c24f8', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert SDRF-Proteomics annotator for crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nExtract the following SDRF metadata terms from the provided publication text and/or PRIDE project description.\nFor each term, return the best available ontology-resolved value with its accession where indicated.\n\nTERMS TO EXTRACT:\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nterm: organism\nsdrf_column: characteristics[organism]\nontology: NCBITaxon (e.g. NCBITaxon:9606)\ndescription: Source species of the proteome of interest in the biological samples analyzed by mass spectrometry. Return the scientific name. \nSource species maybe different than host species when proteins from one organism are expressed in another. \ninstructions: Use NCBITaxon accession. Common examples: Homo sapiens (NCBITaxon:9606),\n  Mus musculus (NCBITaxon:10090), Saccharomyces cerevisiae (NCBITaxon:4932),\n  Escherichia coli (NCBITaxon:562). If multiple species, return a list.\n\nterm: organism part\nsdrf_column: characteristics[organism part]\nontology: UBERON or BTO\ndescription: Main normalized anatomical term for the sample analyzed by mass spectrometry. \ninstructions: Use the clearest ontology-backed anatomy term. Examples: whole-cell lysate, mitochondria,\n  nucleus, cytoplasm, liver, heart, brain, cell, ect. Return null if not applicable\n  (e.g. purified recombinant proteins with no tissue origin).\n\nterm: cell line\nsdrf_column: characteristics[cell line]\nontology: CLO, BTO, or EFO (e.g. CLO:0001230 for HEK293)\ndescription: Name of the cell line if a cell line was used.\ninstructions: Return null if no cell line was used (primary cells or tissues).\n\nterm: cell type\nsdrf_column: characteristics[cell type]\nontology: CL, BTO, or CLO\ndescription: Cell type of the sample.\ninstructions: Return null if not applicable or not stated.\n\nterm: material type\nsdrf_column: characteristics[material type]\nontology: PRIDE:0000837\ndescription: Type of biological material analyzed. Differentiates whether the whole cell lysate was analyzed, just a specific anatomical structure or tissue, or if the samples were purified for specific proteins before mass spectrometry analysis. \nallowed_values: tissue, whole cell lysate, organell, membrane, synthetic, purified proteins, affinity captured\ninstructions: Choose the best matching fixed value. Return null if unclear.\n\nterm: strain or breed\nsdrf_column: characteristics[strain or breed]\nontology: free text\ndescription: Strain, breed, cultivar, or ecotype of the organism.\ninstructions: Return null if not stated.\n\nterm: developmental stage\nsdrf_column: characteristics[developmental stage]\nontology: EFO\ndescription: Developmental stage of the organism or donor.\ninstructions: Return null if not stated or not applicable.\n\nterm: technology type\nsdrf_column: technology type\nontology: PRIDE:0000663\ndescription: Type of technology used. For XL-MS this is always fixed.\nallowed_values: proteomic profiling by mass spectrometry\ninstructions: Always return "proteomic profiling by mass spectrometry" for XL-MS datasets.\n\nterm: source name\nsdrf_column: source name\nontology: PRIDE:0000623\ndescription: Unique identifier for the biological sample. Typically derived from\n  the dataset file names or sample descriptions in the paper.\ninstructions: Describe the naming convention used (e.g. "PXD_stem" or "condition_replicate").\n  This is auto-generated from file names; return a description of the sample naming pattern only.\n\nterm: assay name\nsdrf_column: assay name\nontology: NCIT:C178857\ndescription: Unique identifier for each MS run/assay. Typically the raw file stem.\ninstructions: Describe the naming convention. This is auto-generated; return the pattern only.\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nReturn a single JSON object where each key is the exact sdrf_column string shown above.\nFor each key, return a dict with:\n  "value": the extracted value (string, list, or null)\n  "accession": ontology accession if resolvable (e.g. "NCBITaxon:9606"), else null\n  "confidence": "high", "medium", or "low"\n  "evidence_quote": short verbatim quote from the text supporting the extraction, or null\n  "evidence_location": where in the paper (e.g. "Methods", "Abstract"), or null\n\nExample output structure:\n{\n  "characteristics[organism]": {"value": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae", "accession": "NCBITaxon:4932", "confidence": "high", "evidence_quote": "...", "evidence_location": "Methods"},\n  "characteristics[organism part]": {"value": "mitochondria", "accession": null, "confidence": "high", "evidence_quote": "...", "evidence_location": "Methods"},\n  ...\n}'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '[TITLE]\nLysosome-mediated aggregation of galactose-deficient IgA1 with transferrin receptor 1 links to IgA nephropathy\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nThe retention of galactose-deficient IgA1 (Gd-IgA1) in the mesangium is central to IgA nephropathy (IgAN), but its intracellular fate remains unclear. Here, we show that transferrin receptor 1 (TfR1) mediates Gd-IgA1 uptake into mesangial cell lysosomes, where it forms non-digestible aggregates, disrupts lysosomal function, and triggers inflammatory responses. In renal biopsies from IgAN patients, IgA1 aggregates co-localize with TfR1 within lysosomes. In male mice, TfR1 overexpression enhanced lysosomal accumulation of Gd-IgA1, whereas TfR1 knockdown reduced it. Mechanistically, acidic pH strengthens TfR1–Gd-IgA1 binding via the galactose-deficient hinge region and residue R276. While we acknowledge that sialylation commonly found in patient-derived IgA1 might influence TfR1 binding and that other receptors, such as ASGPR, were not evaluated, our findings nonetheless reveal a lysosome-centered mechanism in IgAN and highlight receptor-mediated retention of Gd-IgA1 as a potential therapeutic target.\nThe retention of galactose-deficient IgA1 (Gd-IgA1) in the mesangium leads to pathogenesis in IgA nephropathy. Here the authors report that Gd-IgA1 is internalized by mesangial cells potentially via transferrin receptor 1, forming aggregates that disrupt lysosomal function and elicit inflammation.\n\n[METHODS]\nMethods\nHuman samples\nCollection of renal biopsy and plasma samples for this study was approved by the ethics committee of Guangdong Provincial People’s Hospital (KY2023-758-01). Written informed on consent was obtained from all study participants. Renal biopsy and plasma were collected from patients with renal biopsy-proven primary IgAN, prior to drug intervention. The healthy control group for plasma sample consisted of participants aged 18–70\u2009years old with no history of autoimmune diseases, tumors, and infectious diseases. Non-tumor renal tissue from patients with kidney cancer used as control. The Clinical and laboratory characteristics of patients with IgAN was listed in Supplementary table\xa01.\nFIB-SEM tomography and 3D reconstruction of lysosomes of mesangial cells\nFocused-ion beam scanning electron microscopy (FIB-SEM) tomography was performed to investigate the ultrastructural organization of mesangial cells in renal tissues. The tissue blocks were initially pre-fixed in a 2% paraformaldehyde (PFA) solution for 2\u2009h. Following pre-fixation, the samples were washed three times in 0.1\u2009M phosphate buffer (PB) to remove any excess fixative. For primary fixation, the samples were incubated in 1% osmium tetroxide (OsO4) solution, which contained 15\u2009mg of potassium ferrocyanide dissolved in 1\u2009mL of 10\u2009g/L osmic acid, at room temperature while shaking for 1\u2009h. After primary fixation, the samples were washed three times with 0.1\u2009M PB and three times with distilled water (ddH2O), with each wash lasting 15\u2009min. Next, the samples were stained with 1% thiocarbohydrazide (TCH) for 30\u2009min at 40\u2009°C in a water bath. Following staining, the samples were washed three times with ddH2O, each wash lasting 15\u2009min. A second fixation step with 1% osmium tetroxide aqueous solution was performed for 1\u2009h, followed by three more washes with ddH2O, each lasting 15\u2009min. For contrast enhancement, the samples were stained with 1% uranyl acetate aqueous solution overnight at 4\u2009°C. After staining, the samples were washed three times with ddH2O, each wash lasting 15\u2009min. Subsequently, the samples were stained with lead aspartate solution for 1\u2009h at 60\u2009°C in a water bath, followed by four washes with ddH2O, each lasting 15\u2009min. The samples were then dehydrated through a graded series of ethanol solutions (30%, 50%, 70%, 85%, and 95%) with each step lasting 15\u2009min. This was followed by two rounds of dehydration in 100% ethanol, each lasting 30\u2009min, and two rounds of dehydration in pure acetone, each lasting 30\u2009min. Finally, the samples were infiltrated with Epon 812 epoxy resin, starting with a 3:7 ratio of resin to pure acetone for 4\u2009h, followed by a 7:3 ratio of resin to acetone for another 4\u2009h. The samples were then infiltrated with pure resin three times, each for 4\u2009h, and polymerized in an oven at 60\u2009°C for 48\u2009h. The surfaces of the resin-embedded tissues were exposed using a diamond knife on an Ultracut UCT (Leica Biosystems). The block was mounted onto an HV-8 aluminum stub (Micro Star, Tokyo, Japan). The space between the block and the stub was filled with carbon paste and then coated with a thin layer of platinum-palladium using an MC1000 ion sputter coater (Hitachi High-Technologies).\nThe surfaces of the resin-embedded tissues were exposed using a diamond knife on a Leica EM UC7(Leica Biosystems). The block was mounted onto an 45eMetal Block. The space between the block and the stub was filled with carbon paste and then coated with a thin layer of platinum-palladium using an Q150R S sputtering instrument(Quorum Technologies Ltd, London, UK).\nThe coated surface of the block was imaged with a Helios G4 UC FIB-SEM (Thermo Fisher Scientific, Waltham, MA, United States) at a high acceleration voltage of 10\u2009kV to determine the area of interest. To prevent beam damage, a platinum layer was deposited on the area of interest using a 2.5\u2009nA beam current, where gallium ions were accelerated by a voltage of 30\u2009kV. A trench was cut using FIB milling with a 25\u2009nA beam current, also at 30\u2009kV, to create a new imaging surface. Serial FIB-SEM images were acquired using Auto Slice and View imaging software (Thermo Fisher Scientific). New surfaces for serial block-face imaging were generated using FIB milling with a 0.77\u2009nA beam current.\n3D Reconstruction: The alignment of serial FIB-SEM images, segmentation of target mesangial cells, and 3D reconstruction were performed using an independently developed artificial intelligence program. 1. Automatic Segmentation with UNet: we have implemented the UNet network for the training of input data, achieving the automatic segmentation of primary tissue structures. This sophisticated deep learning model has been pivotal in delineating the intricate cellular architectures with high fidelity. 2. Isosurface Extraction via Marching Cubes: Following segmentation, we applied the marching cubes algorithm to the resultant cell structure masks to extract three-dimensional isosurface information. This methodology has been essential in capturing the volumetric contours, providing a detailed three-dimensional representation of the cellular structures. 3. Iterative Smoothing and Mesh Simplification: To refine the three-dimensional isosurfaces, we employed Taubin’s smoothing and mesh simplification techniques. These iterative processes have been key in reducing noise and complexity, enhancing the accuracy and visual quality of the reconstructed models.4. Three-Dimensional Visualization with VTK: The final step in our pipeline involves the use of the Visualization Toolkit (VTK) for rendering the smoothed models. VTK serves as a powerful engine for three-dimensional visualization, enabling us to present the cellular structures in a compelling and informative manner.\nImmunoelectron microscopy (IEM)\nIEM samples were processed and examined by Experimental Pathology Laboratory at KingMed Diagnostics. Tissue sampling and fixation: Renal biopsy samples were strip-like with a diameter of 1\u2009millimeter. Tissues were fixed in a 0.5% glutaraldehyde\u2009+\u20094% paraformaldehyde mixed fixative for at least 4\u2009h at 4\u2009°C. (Alfa Aesar, Catalog No. A17876)\nWashing and post-fixation: Tissues were washed with 0.1\u2009M phosphate buffer (PB) six times, for 30\u2009min each time, and post-fixed with 1% osmium tetroxide (prepared by dissolving 1\u2009g of osmium tetroxide crystals (TED PELLA, Catalog No. 18456) for in 0.1\u2009M PB 1.5–2\u2009h at 4\u2009°C. Following post-fixation, tissues were washed with 0.1\u2009M PB three times, for 10\u2009min each time.\nGradient dehydration and resin infiltration: Tissues underwent gradient dehydration using ethanol and acetone as follows: 30%, 50%, 70%, 90%, and 100% ethanol (10\u2009min each), followed by a 1:1 mixture of 100% ethanol and 100% acetone (15\u2009min), and 100% acetone (twice, 15\u2009min each). Tissues were infiltrated with Epon812 resin (TED PELLA, Catalog No. GP18010) in a 1:1 mixture with 100% acetone for 3-4\u2009h at 38\u2009°C, followed by a 1:2 mixture for another 3-4\u2009h at 38\u2009°C, and finally with pure Epon812 resin for 3–4\u2009h at 38\u2009°C.\nEmbedding and polymerization: Tissues were embedded in pure Epon812 resin using embedding molds and polymerized in an electric heating drying oven (model: DHG-9075A) at 38\u2009°C for 6\u2009h, 60\u2009°C for 6\u2009h, and 80\u2009°C for 12\u2009h.\nSemi-thin sectioning and localization: Semi-thin sections (1\u2009μm thick) were cut using an ultramicrotome (Leica UC-7) and observed under an optical microscope (Olympus BX41) to locate suitable areas for electron microscopy observation. Renal tissue containing glomeruli was selected for ultrathin sectioning.\nUltrathin sectioning and immunoelectron microscopy labeling: Ultrathin sections (70-90\u2009nm thick) were cut using the ultramicrotome (Leica UC-7) and mounted on 200-mesh nickel grids. Immunoelectron microscopy labeling was performed with primary antibodies (IgA1 and LAMP1, diluted 1:100 in 1% BSA) overnight at 4\u2009°C. Excess primary antibodies were washed with 0.01\u2009M Phosphate-Buffered Saline (PBS), and gold-conjugated secondary antibodies (diluted 1:20 in 1% BSA; EMS, Catalog No. 25109) were applied and incubated for 80\u2009min at 37\u2009°C. Sections were rinsed with double-distilled water to remove excess antibodies.\nDouble staining with uranyl acetate and lead citrate: Sections were stained with 2% uranyl acetate (prepared by dissolving 2\u2009g of uranyl acetate in 100\u2009mL of 70% ethanol; supplied by EMS, Catalog No. 22400) for 30\u2009min at room temperature, followed by rinsing with double-distilled water and drying. Staining with 3% lead citrate (TED PELLA, Catalog No. 19314) prepared by dissolving 3\u2009g of lead citrate in 100\u2009mL of freshly boiled double-distilled water, adding 0.1\u2009mL of 10\u2009mol/L NaOH, and shaking until the solution clears was performed for 15\u2009min at room temperature, followed by rinsing with double-distilled water and drying.\nObservation with transmission electron microscope: Samples were observed using a transmission electron microscope (model: JEM-1400, produced by JEOL Ltd., Tokyo, Japan) at an accelerating voltage of 80\u2009kV. Sections were located at low magnification on the fluorescent screen, and the CCD camera (model: MORADA, brand: EMSIS) was inserted. The Radius ALL 2.2 (Build 21230) software was used for imaging and measurement. Microscope settings were adjusted for clear images, and images were acquired.\nCRISPR-Cas9 screening\nFirstly, to generate SV40 MES13-Cas9 overexpression cell lines, lentiviruses were produced by transfecting 293\u2009T cells with Lenti-CRISPR-V2 Cas9-GFP, psPAX2 and VSV-G plasmids. SV40 MES13 cells were infected with lentivirus and GFP+ cells were selected by fluorescence-activated cell sorting. Secondly, to generate SV40 MES13 sgRNA library cell lines, retroviruses were produced by transfecting platE cells with pMXs sgRNA-library-BFP, pcl-10Al plasmids. SV40 MES13-Cas9 cells were infected with retrovirus and the virus titer was controlled to be <30%. Thirdly, the infected SV40 MES13 cells were collected and stained with 100\u2009ug/ml Gd-IgA1-AF647 at 4°C for 30\u2009min. Then, the 10% AF647+ or AF647- cells with Lenti-CRISPR-V2 Cas9-GFP and sgRNA-library-BFP double positive cell populations were sorted after washed by PBS. The GFP and BFP double positive cells were collected as input control. Finally, cell DNA was extracted and high-throughput sequencing was performed to detect the abundance of different sgRNA. The top 10 genes with the smallest P-values were selected as candidates. The P-values were obtained using the Model-based Analysis of Genome-wide CRISPR/Cas9 Knockout (MAGeCK) method for prioritizing single-guide RNAs, genes, and pathways in genome-scale CRISPR/Cas9 knockout screens.\nsiRNA-mediated knockdown of genes in mesangial cells\nWe designed and synthesized siRNA oligonucleotides (provided by Tsingke Biotech Co., Ltd.) and used a non-targeting sequence as a negative control. Transfection was performed using Lipofectamine RNAiMAX transfection reagent on cultured human mesangial cells. Prior to transfection, cells were seeded at 6\u2009×\u2009105 in 6-well plates to ensure they reached ~80% confluence at the time of transfection. The siRNA-containing transfection complexes were prepared at 100\u2009nM and added to the cell culture medium. Forty-eight hours post-transfection, the mRNA levels of genes were analyzed by real-time quantitative PCR (qRT-PCR) to validate the knockdown efficiency.\nSequence of siRNAs\nCell culture\nMouse mesangial cell line SV40 MES 13 was purchased from ATCC. The base medium for this cell line is a 3:1 mixture of Dulbecco’s Modified Eagle’s Medium (Gibco) and Ham’s F12 medium (Gibco), both supplemented with 14\u2009mM HEPES and fetal bovine serum at concentration of 5%. Human mesangial cells were isolated from human renal tissue and cryopreserved at passage one at 5\u2009×\u2009105 cells / mL (ScienCell, #4200). It was cultured with mesangial cell medium (MsCM, Cat. #4201). Incubate the cells in a humidified incubator at 37\u2009°C with 5% CO2 to maintain optimal pH levels. Change the medium every 2–3\u2009days to provide fresh nutrients and remove metabolic waste. Monitor the cells for confluence and passage them when they reach about 80-90% confluence to avoid overgrowth.\nSuper-resolution live cell imaging and organelle labeling\nThe coding sequences of TFRC was subcloned into the pIRES2-EGFP vector to construct the corresponding plasmids. Plasmids (3000\u2009ng) were diluted with Lipofectamine® 2000 transfection reagent and incubated with human mesangial cells for 4\u2009h before washed. RAB5A, RAB7A and LAMP1 was labeled by CellLight™ Early Endosomes-GFP(C10586), CellLight™ Late Endosomes-GFP(C10588) and CellLight™ Lysosomes-GFP(C10596), respectively. Normal glycosylated control IgA1 (HC-IgA1) and Gd-IgA1 were fluorescently labeled with Alexa Fluor 647. After balancing the protein concentration and fluorescence intensity, mesangial cells were incubated with Gd-IgA1-Alexa Fluor 647 or HC-IgA1-Alexa Fluor 647. The localization of the proteins within mesangial cell organelles was observed using a HIS-SIM super-resolution live-cell imaging system. The fluorescence intensity was calculated using ImageJ software.\nConstruction of TFRC knockdown and overexpression mesangial cells\nTo generate virus for infection of mesangial cells, 2\u2009×\u2009105 HEK293T cells were seeded into the well of a six-well plate. Twenty-four hours later, 2\u2009µg pLKO.1-GFP-shTfrc or 2\u2009µg pHAGE-Tfrc-IRES-ZsGreen, 1.5\u2009µg psPAX2 and 0.5\u2009µg pMD2.G were mixed into 200\u2009µL serum-free DMEM. In another Eppendorf tube, 200\u2009µL serum-free DMEM and 8\u2009µL PEI were mixed, and incubate at room temperature for 10\u2009min. Then, combine the contents of the two tubes and incubate for an additional 20\u2009min. Then, added the mixture to the six-well plate and mix gently. Incubate the plate in the incubator for 6\u2009h, then replace the medium with a complete medium. Fourty-eight hours after transfection, collect the first batch of virus supernatant and add complete medium into the six-well plate. After 24\u2009h, the second virus supernatant was collected. The supernatant should be centrifuge at 350\u2009g for 10\u2009min before infecting mesangial cells. Twenty-four hours before the infection, seeded 2\u2009×\u2009105 cells per well in a six-well plate. Mixed the virus solution with complete medium at a 1:1 ratio (~3\u2009mL) and added it to the wells. After 12\u2009h of infection, the medium was replaced with fresh complete medium. Twelve hours after the replacement with fresh medium, the second infection was performed by adding the mixed virus solution again. Once the cells reached 1\u2009×\u2009108, they were subjected to flow cytometric sorting (excitation wavelength 488\u2009nm, emission wavelength 530\u2009nm, Sony SA3800).\nInhibition of TfR1 in mesangial cells using Ferristatin II\nMesangial cells were cultured in serum-free medium for 6\u2009h and then treated with 50\u2009μM Ferristatin II (Cayman, #36621). Cells were incubated at 37\u2009°C with 5% CO2 for 6\u2009h. PBS was used as the vehicle control.\nDetection of protein aggregates in mesangial cells\nDetection of aggregates in mesangial cells was conducted using the PROTEOSTAT® Aggresome Detection Kit (ENZ-51035, Enzo). This kit employs a 488\u2009nm excitable red fluorescent dye to detect misfolded proteins within aggresomes. Human mesangial cells were grown on confocal dish to achieve 80% confluency. Cells were treated with 0.5\u2009mg/mL Gd-IgA1 or HC-IgA1 for 4\u2009h before washed by 1\u2009×\u2009PBS for three times. Cells treated with PBS alone were served as negative control. Following treatment, cells were washed twice with PBS, and 1000\u2009μL of 4% formaldehyde was applied. The cells were incubated for 30\u2009min at room temperature. After fixation, cells were washed twice with PBS, and 500\u2009μL of permeabilizing solution (0.5% Triton X-100, 3\u2009mM EDTA, pH 8.0) was applied. Following permeabilization, excess buffer was removed, 1\u2009μL of PROTEOSTAT® Aggresome Detection Reagent in 2\u2009mL assay buffer was added to the cover the cells. Samples were protected from light and incubated for 30\u2009min at room temperature. After incubation, the cells were subjected to analysis using a ZEISS LSM 900 confocal microscope, which was set to excite fluorescence at a wavelength of 550\u2009nanometers.\nLysosomal activity assay\nTo evaluate the effect of Gd-IgA1 on lysosomal function in mesangial cells, we utilized a lysosomal intracellular activity assay kit (Abcam, catalog no. ab234622). In brief, mesangial cells were incubated with media containing either a positive control (10\u2009μM Cytochalasin D), Gd-IgA1 (1\u2009mg/mL), or PBS for 1\u2009h at 37\u2009°C with 5% CO2. Following incubation, the media was discarded and the cells were refreshed with new medium with 5% FBS. Then, 15\u2009µL Self-Quenched substrate was added per milliliter of medium to both experimental and control wells. Cells were further incubated for 1\u2009h at 37\u2009°C with 5% CO2. To terminate the enzymatic reaction, cells were washed twice with 1\u2009mL of ice-cold assay buffer. Finally, the cells were analyzed by flow cytometry using a 488\u2009nm excitation filter.\nMultiplex ELISA array of cytokines secreted by mesangial cells\nMesangial cells were incubated with Gd-IgA1 or HC-IgA1 (1\u2009mg/mL) for 24\u2009h. Following this incubation period, the medium was replaced with fresh medium, and the cells were allowed to incubate for an additional 24\u2009h. The conditioned medium was then collected and analyzed using a multiplex ELISA array that included a panel of 120 cytokines (Human Cytokine Array GS2000, RayBiotech Life, Inc.) following the manufacturer’s protocol.\nHistology and RNAscope in situ hybridization\nParaffin-embedded human kidney sections (5\u2009μm thickness) were prepared by a routine procedure. RNAscope in situ hybridization were performed using Probe-Hs-TFRC (cat#437351), Probe- Hs-ACTA2 (cat#1083431), and the RNAscope Multiplex Fluorescent V2 Assay kit (all from Advanced Cell Diagnostics, ACD). Slides were baked at 60°C for 30\u2009min. Slides were incubated in xylene for 10\u2009min twice and 100% ethanol for 2\u2009min twice at room temperature. Slides were airdried and incubated with 10% H2O2 for 10\u2009min at room temperature. After rinsing in distilled water three times, slides were incubated in ACD heat retrieval buffer for 5\u2009min at 95\u2009°C. Slides were rinsed in distilled water three times and incubated in 100% ethanol for 3\u2009min at room temperature. Slides were air dried and incubated with plus protease for 30\u2009min at 40\u2009°C in a humidified slide moat. Samples were incubated with RNAscope probes for 2\u2009h at 40\u2009°C. Slides were washed twice in wash buffer for 2\u2009min at room temperature after probe incubation and between each amplification step. Samples were incubated sequentially with Amp1 for 30\u2009min at 40\u2009°C, Amp2 for 30\u2009min at 40\u2009°C, Amp3 for 15\u2009min at 40\u2009°C, HRP-C1 for 15\u2009min at 40\u2009°C, TSA Plus FITC diluted 1:1000 in TSA buffer for 30\u2009min at 40\u2009°C, and HRP blocker for 15\u2009min at 40\u2009°C, HRP-C2 for 15\u2009min at 40\u2009°C, TSA Plus Cy3 diluted 1:1000 in TSA buffer for 30\u2009min at 40\u2009°C, and HRP blocker for 15\u2009min at 40\u2009°C. Images were examined semi-blind by two renal researchers and consensus was reached.\nPreparation and characterization of HC-IgA1 and Gd-IgA1\nNormal human serum was collected to purify IgA protein. The purification of IgA and Gd-IgA1 was performed as previously described. Briefly, HC-IgA1 was purified from normal human serum. Serum samples of healthy individuals were first diluted with 10\u2009times PBS, and then incubated with Capture SelectTM IgA-CH1 (Hu) affinity matrix (194311010; Thermo Scientific) at 4 °C for 1\u2009h. After that, the beads were collected by a 50\u2009mL gravity column and washed with 60-100\u2009mL PBS. 0.1\u2009M glycine (pH\u2009=\u20093.0). Gd-IgA1 protein was obtained by enzymatic digestion of IgA1 derived from the healthy control, a well-established approach for generating Gd-IgA1. 1\u2009mg of purified IgA1 proteins were treated with 4000 U α2-3,6,8 neuraminidase (P0720L; New England Biolabs), and 400 U β1-3 galactosidase (P0726S; New England Biolabs) in PBS, pH 7.4, at 37 °C for 2\u2009h. Gd-IgA1 was then subjected to dialysis against PBS to remove the glycosidase. The purity of both proteins was assessed using Coomassie Brilliant Blue staining and western blot.\nHC-IgA1 and Gd-IgA1 samples were prepared in PBS, pH 7.4. Size-exclusion chromatography (SEC) was performed using a Superdex 200 Increase 10/300 GL column (GE Healthcare) on a cytiva AKTA pure system, with PBS as the mobile phase at a flow rate of 0.6\u2009mL/min. The column was equilibrated for at least three column volumes at 4°C before injection of 500\u2009μL sample volume. Protein detection was carried out at 280\u2009nm, and fractions were collected manually.\nImmunofluorescence staining and confocal microscopy\nKidney tissues were embedded in OCT compound and sectioned into 5\u2009µm thick slides. The sections were fixed with 4% PFA for 15\u2009min at room temperature. Following a 1\u2009h blocking step with blocking buffer (12411S; Cell Signaling Technology), the slides were incubated with primary antibodies at 4\u2009°C overnight, and then secondary antibodies for 1\u2009h at room temperature. The stained slides were examined using a ZEISS LSM 900 confocal microscope. The antibodies used were as follows:\nImmunohistochemical staining\nSlides were baked at 60 °C for 30\u2009min, then incubated in xylene twice for 15\u2009min each, 100% ethanol for 5\u2009min twice, 95% ethanol for 5\u2009min, 90% ethanol for 5\u2009min, 80% ethanol for 5\u2009min, 75% ethanol for 5\u2009min, 70% ethanol for 5\u2009min, 50% ethanol for 5\u2009min at room temperature slides were incubated in EDTA retrieval buffer (ZLI-9072; Zsbio) for 15\u2009min at 100°C. After washing with distilled water, the slides were incubated with 10% H2O2 for 10\u2009min at room temperature. Following a 30-min blocking step with blocking buffer, the slides were incubated with primary anti-TfR (dilution 1:100; HPA028598; Sigma) overnight at 4\u2009°C, and staining with secondary antibodies (GK600710; Gene Tech) for 1\u2009h at room temperature. The slides were also stained with 3,3’-diaminobenzidine (DAB) to visualize the expression. Visualization was performed using a HAMAMATSU laser scanning microscope.\nWestern blot analysis\nCells or kidney tissues were lysed on ice with radioimmunoprecipitation assay buffer (89900; Thermo scientific) and protease and phosphatase inhibitor (A32961; Thermo scientific) in PBS. The supernatants were collected after centrifugation at 12000\u2009g for 10\u2009min at 4°C. Protein expression was analyzed by western blot analysis. The antibodies used were anti-TfR (dilution 1:1000; ab214039; Abcam), anti-Cathepsin B (dilution 1:1000; 12216-1; Proteintech), anti-Cathepsin D (dilution 1:1000; 21327-1; Proteintech), anti-TFEB (dilution 1:1000; 68632-1; Proteintech), anti-β-actin (dilution 1:5000; 66009-1-Ig; Proteintech) and anti-Rabbit IgG (dilution 1:5000; 111-035-003; Jackson).\nMice model\nAll animal procedures were in accordance with the guidelines and approved by the Ethics Committee of Guangdong Provincial People’s Hospital. Eight-week-old male BALB/c Nude mice were used in this study. Mice were randomly assigned to experimental or control groups with block randomization method. In the overexpression experiment, TfR1 was overexpressed in nude mice by intravenous injection of AAV9-Tfrc or AAV9-CTL virus (2\u2009×\u20091010\u2009GC/g bodyweight). In the knockdown experiment, TfR1 was knocked down using AAV9-shTfrc or AAV9-shCTL virus (4\u2009×\u20091010\u2009GC/g bodyweight). Only male mice were used in this study, as the interaction between TfR1 and Gd-IgA1 is not expected to differ significantly between male and female mice. Mice were group-housed under a 12:12\u2009h light/dark cycle with ad libitum access to food and water.\nReal-time in vivo fluorescence imaging for evaluating the kinetics of Gd-IgA1\nEight-week-old male BALB/c Nude mice were intravenously administered with 200\u2009μL Indocyanine green (ICG) labeled protein at the concentration of 2.5\u2009mg/mL, and the molar ratio of ICG and protein in the labeling process was 5: 1. Real-time monitoring of the biodistribution of ICG-labeled human Gd- IgA1 was performed by IVIS Spectrum In Vivo Imaging System (Perkin Elmer, USA). The excitation and emission filters are 745\u2009nm and 840\u2009nm, respectively. The exposure time is 2\u2009s. The binning level of the images is 1. Scanning range mode is D (20\u2009×\u200920\u2009cm2). Fluorescence images of mice were captured before injection and at different time points post-injection. The fluorescence signal of kidney was analyzed by the manufacturer’s quantitative analysis system.\nConstruct preparations and protein purification\nPlasmid construction. Extracellular domain of human TfR1 gene (101-760; Uniprot-P02786) was synthesized and cloned into a pFastbac1 vector by the Anhui General Biol Co. China for TfR1 (TfR1) protein expression. The N-terminal of TfR1 contains a GP67 signal peptide, and C-terminal with 6\u2009×\u2009His tag.\nThe heavy constant α-chain sequence of IgA1 for the CH1, CH2 and CH3 domains was taken from SWISSPROT (Unipro-P01876-1), and heavy variable α-chain domain (VH) and light chain (VL and CL) sequences were taken from the crystal structure of human TR1.9 IgG1 Fab (PDB code: 1vge). For recombinant IgA1 purification, a 3\u2009×\u2009Flag tag (DYKDHDGDYKDHDIDYKDDDDKLVPRGS) after the HIV signal peptide (MDAMKRGLCCVLLLCGAVFVSPAGA) was added to the N-terminal of the heavy constant α-chain, as previous reported. Residue numbering is based on the recombinant IgA1 containing an N-terminal FLAG tag.\n>IgA1 heavy chain\nDYKDHDGDYKDHDIDYKDDDDKLVPRGSQVKLLEQSGAEVKKPGASVKVSCKASGYSFTS 60 YGLHWVRQAPGQRLEWMGWISAGTGNTKYSQKFRGRVTFTRDTSATTAYMGLSSLRPEDT 120 AVYYCARDPYGGGKSEFDYWGQGTLVTVSSASPTSPKVFPLSLCSTQPDGNVVIACLVQG 180 FFPQEPLSVTWSESGQGVTARNFPPSQDASGDLYTTSSQLTLPATQCLAGKSVTCHVKHY 240 TNPSQDVTVPCPVPSTPPTPSPSTPPTPSPSCCHPRLSLHRPALEDLLLGSEANLTCTLT 300 GLRDASGVTFTWTPSSGKSAVQGPPERDLCGCYSVSSVLPGCAEPWNHGKTFTCTAAYPE 360 SKTPLTATLSKSGNTFRPEVHLLPPPSEELALNELVTLTCLARGFSPKDVLVRWLQGSQE 420 LPREKYLTWASRQEPSQGTTTFAVTSILRVAAEDWKKGDTFSCMVGHEALPLAFTQKTID 480 RLAGKPTHVNVSVVMAEVDGTCY 503\nRecombinant TfR1 expression and purification\nSf9 cells (Spodoptera frugiperda) were cultured in serum-free insect cell culture media (Union-Biotech, UK1000) at 27\u2009°C, 120 round per minute (rpm) and passaged every 2\u2009days and for no more than 20 passages. Bac-viruses were prepared following protocol of the Bac-to-BacTM baculovirus expression system (Thermo Fisher Scientific, 10359016). Briefly, the pFast bac1 plasmid constructed TfR1 genes were firstly transformed into E. coli strain DH10bac cells for transposition into the bacmid. The recombinant bacmid was isolated and was mixed with cellfectin with regent (Thermo Fisher Scientific, 10362100), then the mixture was used to transfected sf9 cells for passage 1 recombinant bac-virus (P1 virus) preparation. The P1 virus was then used as seed to amplify the virus stock for another two rounds. Finally, passage 3 (P3) bac-virus was used for protein expression.\nFor TfR1 expression, sf9 cells were seeded at a density of 2.5\u2009×\u2009106, and 0.25% P3 virus was added. After 3\u2009days suspension culture, the supernatant was collected by 18,000\u2009g centrifugation at 4°C. 2\u2009mL Ni-NTA beads were added to the culture medium, and then incubated by 500\u2009rpm stirring for 2\u2009h at 4°C. All beads were collected by a 50\u2009mL gravity-column, and washed by buffer A (20\u2009mM Hepes, 150\u2009mM NaCl, 0.5\u2009mM DTT) containing gradient concentration of imidazole for 20\u2009mM to 50\u2009mM, and eluted by buffer A containing 250\u2009mM imidazole. The elution was concentrated by a 50\u2009kDa cutoff concentrator and loaded onto a Superose 6 Increase 10/300 GL (Cytiva, 29091596) in buffer A. The TfR1 in peaks was collected and freeze quickly by liquid nitrogen and stored in −80°C for further using.\nRecombinant IgA1 expression and purification\nHEK293F cells was maintained in serum-free mammalian cells culture medium (Union-Biotech, UK1001). To express IgA1 protein, the HEK293F cells was diluted to 2.5\u2009×\u2009106/mL in fresh medium. For IgA1 preparation, plasmids containing equal amount of heavy and light chain were mixed with PEI at a ratio of 1:3 in medium, and added to cell culture for protein expression. In the 20th hour of transfection, 10\u2009mM sodium butyrate was supplemented to enhance protein expression. After 5\u2009days culture, the supernatant was collected by 18,000\u2009g centrifugation at 4°C, and IgA1 proteins were purified from medium by the Flag beads (Genscript, L00432). The elute was concentrated and further purified by a Superdex 200 Increase 10/300 GL (Cytiva, 17517501) in buffer A.\nCo-Immunoprecipitation assay\nAbout 50\u2009μg TfR1 protein was incubated with 50\u2009μL Ni-NTA beads in buffer A at room temperature (RT) for 20\u2009mins, and the beads were washed with buffer A for three times to remove unbound protein. Next, 200\u2009μg purified total IgA1 was added to the beads, and the volume was filled to 400\u2009μL with buffer A (pH7.2). The mixture was incubated on a turbosonic at RT for 2\u2009h. After that, a 500\u2009g centrifuge was made to collect the supernatant (flow-through), and using 400\u2009μL buffer A (pH7.2) to wash beads, then made another round of centrifuge to collect washing solution. Repeated washing steps for twice, and added 400\u2009μL elution buffer (buffer A containing 200\u2009mM imidazole) to elute bound proteins, collected the supernatant after centrifugation. 50\u2009μL sample from each step was taken for SDS-PAGE and western Blot analysis.\nWhen pH 5.5 condition was tested, the buffer A (pH 7.2) was replaced by buffer A (pH 5.5), and other operations remained unchanged.\nSurface plasmon resonance (SPR) assay\nPurified TfR1 protein was used for directly detect affinity parameters of TfR1 and various types of Gd-IgA1 or hinge peptides by surface plasmon resonance (SPR). Filtered Hepes buffer (20\u2009mM Hepes, pH7.5, 150\u2009mM NaCl) was used as running buffer throughout the determination. If different pH conditions were tested, the running buffer was replaced and filled the system before starting tests. After the CM5 chip (Cytiva, 29149604) was loaded and cleaned by 50\u2009mM NaOH and activated by EDC/NHS mixture, ~12, 000 RU of TfR1 (0.05\u2009mg/mL diluted in pH4.5 50\u2009mM acetic acid buffer) was captured on channel 2. The channel 1 was untreated and used as reference channel. For the quick binding test, the Gd-IgA1 proteins were diluted to 0.25\u2009μM with selected running buffer, and 1\u2009μM transferrin was used as positive control, and 10\u2009μM BSA and 10\u2009μM β−1.3-galactosidase was used as negative control. The time of samples injection was 120\u2009s, and disassociate 120\u2009s. When peptide ligands were tested, the peptides were diluted to 50\xa0μM for sampling, and 50\xa0μM Flag peptide used as negative control. For kinetic binding test, the ligands were diluted to a series of concentrations for general procedure. Four steps were included, channel washing 60\u2009s, ligand association 120\u2009s, disassociation 480\u2009s and regeneration 120\u2009s, with flow rate 10\u2009μL/min, temperature 25°C. A kinetic analysis of each ligand/analyte interaction was obtained by fitting the response data (containing 6-8 gradients of ligand concentrations) to a 1:1 bimolecular interaction mode (Ka, fit global; Kd, fit global; Ramx, fit global; tc, fit global; R1, constant; Drift, constant).\nLiquid Chromatography Tandem Mass Spectrometry\nSamples preparation: purified TfR1 protein was mixed with Gd-IgA1 at a molar ratio of 1:3 in buffer A (pH 5.5) and incubated at room temperature for 2\u2009hours. After that, the 1-Ethyl-3-(3-dimethylaminopropyl) carbodiimide (EDC) and N-Hydroxysulfosuccinimide (sulfo-NHS) solutions (Cytiva, BR100050) were mixed with a ratio of (1:5, v: v) and transferred to the protein mixture at a ratio of (1:10, v: v) drop by drop. The sample continued to incubate under room temperature for another 2\u2009hours, and then equal volume of 100\u2009mM tris buffer (pH 5.5) was used to end the crosslinking reaction. 100\u2009μL samples (about 50-60\u2009μg proteins) were denatured by adding 20\u2009μL 5\u2009×\u2009loading buffer and treated in 100 °C heating, and then separated by SDS-PAGE with a gradient gel (4-15%). The Gd-IgA1 and TfR1 crosslinked band in the gel was cut and analyzed by mass spectrometry.\nLiquid Chromatography Tandem Mass Spectrometry (LC-MS/MS) on an Orbitrap Fusion Lumos instrument (Thermo Fisher Scientific) with an High-Energy Collisional Dissociation (HCD) fragmentation function was employed for LC-MS/MS analyses. The gel samples were firstly cut into little pieces (\u2009~\u20091 mm3) and incubated in 50\u2009mM NH4HCO3/acetonitrile solution (1:1, v: v) to destain. Then the gel was washed using 100% acetonitrile for 2 times and dehydrated in vacuum. The samples were treated with DTT and IAA to reductive alkylation. Protein digested using trypsin overnight, and peptides were recollected and concentrated using a vacuum concentrator. In LC-MS/MS analysis, digested products were separated by a 120\u2009min gradient elution at a flow rate 0.300\u2009µL/min with the Thermo Ultimate 3000 nano-UPLC system which was directly interfaced with the Thermo Fusion LUMOS mass spectrometer. The analytical column was an Acclaim PepMap RSLC column (75\u2009µm ID, 250\u2009mm length, C18). Mobile phase A consisted of 0.1% formic acid, and mobile phase B consisted of 100% acetonitrile and 0.1% formic acid. The Fusion LUMOS mass spectrometer was operated in the data-dependent acquisition mode using Xcalibur 4.1.50 software and there is a single full-scan mass spectrum in the Orbitrap (375−1500\u2009m/z, 60,000 resolution) followed by data-dependent MS/MS scans. The MS/MS spectra from each LC-MS/MS run were searched against the selected database using the software Byonic.\nSynthesis of GalNAc modified hinge peptide of IgA1\nA 38\u2009mer peptide (-DYKDDDDKGSGSPVPSTPPTPSPSTPPTPSPSCCHPRR-) covering the hinge region of IgA1 was synthesized. This peptide was glycosylated with GalNAc and synthesized on Wang resin from the C-terminus to the N-terminus following solid phase peptide synthesis (SPPS) protocols and was commercially obtained from MedChemExpress. The glycosylated peptide was validated by mass spectrometry.\nStatistics\nQuantitative data are displayed as mean\u2009±\u2009SD. Statistical differences were determined using a Student’s t-test for two groups or one-way analysis of variance (ANOVA) followed by Dunnett’s post hoc test for multiple comparisons. Nonparametric data were analyzed by Mann-Whitney test. Statistical analyses were performed using SPSS Statistics version 23.\nReporting summary\nFurther information on research design is available in the\xa0Nature Portfolio Reporting Summary linked to this article.\n\n[RESULTS]\nResults\nIgA1 retention was found in lysosomes of mesangial cells in patients with IgAN\nTo obtain a comprehensive spatial landscape of mesangial cells and IgA1 deposition in the kidneys of IgAN patients, we conducted volumetric electron microscopy (EM) on renal tissues from IgAN patients. Conventional renal EM pathology involves sampling at an extremely limited plane, with EM sections being 80\u2009nanometers thick and glomerular diameters measuring 200 micrometers. Each EM image corresponds to roughly a 4 ‱ (permyriad) sampling, often neglecting complete spatial information. To perform volumetric EM, we sectioned at a thickness of 50\u2009nanometers, acquiring 1300 serial sections of images. Through three-dimensional reconstruction, we discovered numerous lysosomes with prominent densities and sizes, providing a more detailed spatial understanding of their distribution and morphology (Fig.\xa01a). We examined the lysosome diameters and density in the renal EM pathology images from IgAN patients and para-tumoral controls, finding that the lysosomes in IgAN patients were larger and denser (Fig.\xa01b). Using serial sections, we performed both transmission EM and IgA1 immuno-EM on the same lysosomal region of the mesangial cells, revealing the deposition of gold-labeled IgA1 within the lysosomes of IgAN patients (Fig.\xa01c). Notably, although it is widely accepted that electron-dense deposits (EDD) formed by IgA1 are located outside the mesangial cells, our immuno-EM analysis revealed that IgA1 proteins can aggregate within the lysosomes of renal mesangial cells, as evidenced by the encapsulation of IgA1 within single-membrane structures that were positively labeled for lysosomal marker LAMP1 by immuno-EM (Fig.\xa01d, e). Immunofluorescence examinations further confirmed the co-localization of the LAMP1 and IgA1 in the mesangial area of IgAN patients (Fig.\xa01f). These results indicate that IgA1 can be retained within the lysosomes of mesangial cells in IgAN patients, offering new spatial distribution insights into IgA1 deposition under pathological conditions of IgAN.\nTfR1 mediates Gd-IgA1 endocytosis by mesangial cells\nTo elucidate the biological events during mesangial cells uptake of IgA1 proteins, we labeled Gd-IgA1 protein with Alexa Fluor® 647 dye (AF647) and labeled the endosomes and lysosomes of mesangial cells with green fluorescent protein (GFP), then incubated them with human mesangial cells. We used Gd-IgA1 because it is recognized as the main pathological IgA1 leading to IgAN, and our previous studies using multi-model intravital imaging showed stronger renal deposition of Gd-IgA1. First, we verified that Gd-IgA1 does not spontaneously form aggregates. Additionally, the size and galactose deficiency of the Gd-IgA1 protein were not affected by conjugation with AF647 (Supplementary Fig.\xa01a–d). Using high-intensity structured illumination microscopy (HIS-SIM), a live-cell super-resolution imaging method, we tracked the localization of Gd-IgA1 in different cellular compartments of mesangial cells. Gd-IgA1 gradually entered early endosomes and late endosomes, then fused with lysosomes and stabilized in lysosomes (Fig.\xa02a). Cellular electron microscopy confirmed a significant increase in lysosomes within mesangial cells following Gd-IgA1 incubation (Fig.\xa02b). This finding is not only consistent with the pathological findings in patients with IgAN, indicating that human mesangial cells have a strong capacity to uptake Gd-IgA1 proteins, but also demonstrates that Gd-IgA1 might be favorable for endocytosis by mesangial cells and hence accumulate within lysosomes.\nThese results prompted us to investigate the mechanism of Gd-IgA1 deposition in the lysosomes of mesangial cells. We employed flow cytometry experiments, which revealed a significantly greater binding of Gd-IgA1 to mesangial cells, compared with IgA1 with normal galactosylation (HC-IgA1), and the binding was concentration-dependent (Fig.\xa02c). This result suggested that the binding of Gd-IgA1 to mesangial cells is mediated by membrane proteins/receptors on mesangial cells. To identify such receptors, we used a CRISPR-Cas9 based screening strategy, utilizing a library containing ~2600 membrane proteins. We obtained a list of candidate genes by sequencing sgRNAs from the population of mesangial cells with the lowest 10% binding affinity to Gd-IgA1 (Fig.\xa02d, e). To identify the most likely candidate, we performed siRNA knockdowns on the receptors in the list, followed by flow cytometry binding assays. We found that Tfrc was among these candidates, and knocking down Tfrc significantly inhibited the binding of Gd-IgA1 to mesangial cells (Fig.\xa02f), suggesting that TfR1 is a critical receptor mediating the interaction between mesangial cells and Gd-IgA1.\nLive-cell imaging revealed that human mesangial cells expressing TfR1-GFP internalized Gd-IgA1 protein, and Gd-IgA1 co-localized with TfR1 in vesicles (Fig.\xa03a). Based on these findings, we generated stable cell lines overexpressing or knocked down for TFRC using human mesangial cells (Fig.\xa03b, Supplementary Fig.\xa02a, b). Over-expression of TfR1 significantly enhanced Gd-IgA1 endocytosis, whereas knocking down TfR1 significantly inhibited this process in human mesangial cells (Fig.\xa03c). We performed a series of experiments with graded knockdown or overexpression of TfR1 in mesangial cells and found that the binding of Gd-IgA1 to mesangial cells was consistent with the levels of TfR1 expression (Fig.\xa03d–g). We introduced the TfR1 inhibitor Ferristatin II to reduce TfR1 expression and found that Ferristatin II significantly inhibited the binding of Gd-IgA1 to mesangial cells (Fig.\xa03h, i). In summary, these findings demonstrate that TfR1 mediates the binding and endocytosis of Gd-IgA1 by mesangial cells.\nAggregation of Gd-IgA1 in the lysosomes triggering inflammatory responses in mesangial cells\nFollowing our observations that TfR1 mediated the endocytosis of Gd-IgA1 into mesangial cells, we next examined the fate of Gd-IgA1 within lysosomes and its impact on cellular function and inflammation. First, we assessed the degradation rate of Gd-IgA1 in lysosomes. After 48\u2009h, mesangial cells degraded HC-IgA1, whereas Gd-IgA1 remained accumulated within the lysosomes (Fig.\xa04a, b). ELISA quantification confirmed a significantly higher concentration of Gd-IgA1 within mesangial cells compared to HC-IgA1 (Fig.\xa04c). Non-reducing immunoblots further demonstrated that intact Gd-IgA1 was retained in mesangial cells (Fig.\xa04d), suggesting impaired degradation of Gd-IgA1 within lysosomes. To further understand the role of TfR1 in this process, we knocked down TfR1 in mesangial cells and observed a significant reduction in the retention of Gd-IgA1 within these cells (Fig.\xa04e–g). Furthermore, Ferristatin II also inhibited the accumulation of Gd-IgA1 in mesangial cells (Fig.\xa04h–j), supporting the hypothesis that TfR1 mediates the entry and subsequent retention of Gd-IgA1 in lysosomes.\nUsing an aggresome detection assay, we observed the formation of spherical and globular protein aggregates within mesangial cells following Gd-IgA1 incubation (Fig.\xa05a). These aggregates co-localized with IgA1, indicating that Gd-IgA1 formed protein aggregates after entry into the lysosomes of mesangial cells (Fig.\xa05b). To assess lysosomal function, we employed a cellular lysosome detection assay, where the fluorescence signal generated by degradation is proportional to intracellular lysosomal activity. Our results showed that the fluorescence signal in mesangial cells treated with Gd-IgA1 was significantly suppressed, indicating that Gd-IgA1 inhibits lysosomal function more effectively than known lysosomal inhibitors (Fig.\xa05c, Supplementary Fig.\xa03). This lysosomal dysfunction was linked to the aggregation of Gd-IgA1, impairing the cell’s ability to degrade these proteins. TFEB, a master regulator of lysosomal biogenesis was significantly reduced by Gd-IgA1 retention (Fig.\xa05d). The levels of the lysosomal protease cathepsin B and cathepsin D were also significantly reduced (Fig.\xa05e). These results indicated that lysosomal function was inhibited by Gd-IgA1 retention.\nIn parallel, an inflammatory cytokine array analysis showed a significant increase in IL6, CXCL8/ IL-8, CCL3/ MIP-1α and TNF-α levels in mesangial cells upon stimulation with Gd-IgA1 (Fig.\xa05f, g). This finding was further validated by ELISA, which demonstrated a significant increase in IL-6 secretion by mesangial cells upon Gd-IgA1 stimulation (Fig.\xa05h). Together, these results suggest that TfR1 mediates the accumulation of Gd-IgA1 in lysosomes, leading to the formation of protein aggregates and subsequent lysosomal dysfunction. This dysfunction triggers inflammatory responses in mesangial cells, suggesting a novel pathogenic mechanism in IgAN.\nTfR1 modulates Gd-IgA1 retention in mesangial cells in vivo\nTo validate our in vitro findings on the role of TfR1 in mediating Gd-IgA1 endocytosis and its subsequent effects in vivo, we conducted experiments involving TfR1 overexpression and knockdown in BALB/c Nude mice. Firstly, we overexpressed TfR1 in nude mice by intravenously injecting an AAV9-Tfrc virus (2\u2009×\u20091010 GC/g bodyweight). Separately, we knocked down TfR1 in mice using an AAV9-shTfrc virus (4\u2009×\u20091010 GC/g bodyweight). The efficiency of TfR1 overexpression and knockdown was confirmed through western blotting and immunofluorescent assessment (Supplementary Fig.\xa04a–d). We then dynamically monitored the intensity of Gd-IgA1 deposition in the kidneys using in vivo fluorescence imaging. A significant increase in Gd-IgA1 deposition was observed in the kidneys of mice overexpressing TfR1, whereas TfR1 knockdown significantly inhibited Gd-IgA1 deposition in the kidneys (Fig.\xa06a, b). Immunofluorescent examinations on frozen sections of renal tissue confirmed an increase in the deposition of Gd-IgA1 in the mesangial cells of mice with TfR1 overexpression (Supplementary Fig.\xa04e). Gd-IgA1 was colocalized with LAMP1 and TfR1, demonstrating the precise subcellular location of Gd-IgA1 deposition within the lysosomes of mesangial cells (Fig.\xa06c). This result suggested that TfR1 enhances the deposition of Gd-IgA1 in the lysosomes of mesangial cells, corroborating our in vitro findings. Subsequently, immunofluorescence staining confirmed reduced deposition of Gd-IgA1 in the TfR1 knockdown group (Fig.\xa06d, Supplementary Fig.\xa04f). These findings reinforced the critical role of TfR1 in mediating the endocytosis and lysosomal deposition of Gd-IgA1 in mesangial cells, as observed in our in vitro studies, demonstrating that TfR1 overexpression exacerbates, while TfR1 knockdown mitigates, Gd-IgA1 accumulation in the mouse model.\nElevated TfR1 expression in IgAN patients and its clinical implications\nTo further elucidate the role of TfR1 in patients with IgAN, we examined TfR1 expression in renal samples from patients with IgAN. Immunofluorescence staining revealed co-localization of TfR1 and IgA1 protein in the mesangium of glomeruli in IgAN patients (Fig.\xa07a). This observation is consistent with our cellular experiments, supporting the notion that TfR1 mediates the binding of IgA1 to mesangial cells in IgAN patients. Immunohistochemistry further demonstrated a significant increase in TfR1 expression in the glomeruli of IgAN patients compared to controls (Fig.\xa07b). This finding aligns with our in vitro data, suggesting that elevated TfR1 expression is a characteristic feature of IgAN.\nUsing RNAscope assay, we identified that mesangial cells express TfR1 mRNA, predominantly in cells positive for alpha-smooth muscle actin (α-SMA), indicating high in situ expression of TfR1 in mesangial cells of IgAN patients (Fig.\xa07c). This high expression of TfR1 mRNA in mesangial cells correlates with our previous findings on its role in mediating Gd-IgA1 endocytosis and deposition. Moreover, we discovered a significant correlation between the expression level of glomerular mesangial TfR1 and urinary protein levels in IgAN patients (R\u2009=\u20090.398, P\u2009<\u20090.0001, Fig.\xa07d, Supplementary table\xa01). This suggests that the degree of TfR1 expression is associated with the severity of kidney damage in these patients, indicating a potential prognostic value of TfR1 expression in IgAN.\nIn summary, these findings in patient samples strengthen the critical role of TfR1 in mediating the endocytosis and lysosomal deposition of Gd-IgA1 in mesangial cells, as demonstrated in our in vitro and mouse models. The significant increase in TfR1 expression and its correlation with disease severity in IgAN patients underscore the potential of targeting TfR1 as a therapeutic strategy.\nAcidic conditions enhance the binding affinity of Gd-IgA1 to TfR1 and promote lysosomal deposition\nTo investigate how Gd-IgA1 interacts with TfR1, we recombinantly expressed and purified IgA1 in HEK293 cells and prepared Gd-IgA1 using glycosidases (including α2-3, 6, 8 neuraminidase, and β1-3 galactosidase). The successful preparation of Gd-IgA1 was confirmed via western blotting with anti-IgA1 and anti-Gd-IgA1 antibodies (Supplementary Fig.\xa05a, b). We then examined the affinity between Gd-IgA1 and TfR1 under various conditions using surface plasmon resonance (SPR). Under physiological conditions (150\u2009mM NaCl, pH 7.2), Gd-IgA1 demonstrated a higher binding capacity to TfR1 compared to HC-IgA1, which is critical for the initial endocytosis process, though this binding was still weaker than that of transferrin (supplementary Table\xa02). Notably, under acidic conditions (pH 5.5), the binding capacity of Gd-IgA1 to TfR1 significantly increased, mirroring the behavior of the positive control, transferrin (Apo state). These findings strongly suggest that acidic conditions play a key role in regulating the Gd-IgA1 and TfR1 interaction. To further explore this, we measured the affinity of Gd-IgA1 to TfR1 under pH 5.5 conditions, finding a binding capacity of KD\u2009=\u20091.09\u2009×\u200910-6 M (Fig.\xa08a). This result indicates the interaction between Gd-IgA1 and TfR1 in acidic environments, likely contributing to the formation of degradation-resistant complexes.\nTo further confirm the clinical relevance of these findings, we incubated TfR1 coupled beads with IgA1 from IgAN patients in 100\u2009mM Hepes buffer at pH 7.2 or pH 5.5. Consistent with the SPR results, more intense IgA1 signal was detected binding to TfR1 beads under pH 5.5, whereas minimal IgA1 binding to TfR1 was detected under pH 7.2 conditions (Supplementary Fig.\xa05c).\nTo understand the impact of acidic conditions on the interaction between Gd-IgA1 and TfR1, we assessed the binding capacity of Gd-IgA1 to TfR1 under a range of acidic pH conditions. Interestingly, as the buffer pH was decreased from 7.2 to 5.0, both the binding levels and stability of Gd-IgA1 progressively increased (supplementary Table\xa03), indicating a continuous increase in affinity between Gd-IgA1 and TfR1 from neutral to acidic conditions. These data support that acidic conditions within lysosomes enable Gd-IgA1 to acquire higher affinity for TfR1, promoting the formation of large complexes (aggregates). This aggregation leads to incomplete degradation and lysosomal deposition of Gd-IgA1. Thus, the increased binding affinity of Gd-IgA1 to TfR1 under acidic conditions highlights a crucial aspect of IgAN pathogenesis.\nNext, we aimed to determine the specific interaction region responsible for Gd-IgA1 and TfR1. We prepared a complex of Gd-IgA1 and extracellular domain of TfR1 by incubating the two proteins under acidic conditions and stabilizing it using the chemical cross-linking agent glutaraldehyde (Fig.\xa08b). A stable Gd-IgA1-TfR1 complex was obtained through size exclusion chromatography (Fig.\xa08c). The peak of this complex was distinct, with molecular weights matching the expected mass, and the peak of the Gd-IgA1-TfR1 complex appeared ahead of the separate peaks of TfR1 or Gd-IgA1. This observation suggests that Gd-IgA1 and TfR1 form a stable complex, and their combined mass is greater than the individual proteins, indicating successful complex formation. Under negative staining transmission electron microscopy, the protein particles of the Gd-IgA1 and TfR1 complex were clear and uniform in response to uranyl salts (Fig.\xa08d). This indicates that the interaction between Gd-IgA1 and TfR1 is stable. More importantly, in western blot analysis, in addition to detecting Gd-IgA1 (heavy chain, ~55\u2009kDa) and extracellular domain of TfR1 (~77\u2009kDa), we detected complex of TfR1 and the heavy chain of Gd-IgA1 (~132\u2009kDa) using anti-Flag antibody (Supplementary Fig.\xa06a). Since deglycosidase removed O-glycosylation from the hinge peptide, these results suggest that TfR1 likely interacted with Gd-IgA1 through the galactose-deficient hinge peptide.\nThe crucial role of the hinge region in Gd-IgA1 binding to TfR1\nTo test the hypothesis that the hinge peptide of Gd-IgA1 is the binding site for TfR1, we excised the gel bands corresponding to the Gd-IgA1-TfR1 complex from the SDS-PAGE gel and analyzed them by high-resolution mass spectrometry (Fig.\xa09a). This analysis identified fragments containing the hinge peptide sequence, with the R276 residue interacting with the E350 and D356 residues in the apical domain of TfR1 (Fig.\xa09b, Supplementary Fig.\xa06b; supplementary Table\xa04). These results reveal that the hinge peptide of Gd-IgA1 interacts specifically with the apical domain of TfR1.\nTo further investigate the impact of the specific site R276 near the IgA1 hinge peptide on its binding to TfR1, we prepared two types of hinge peptides. The short hinge peptide (short-HP) included only the hinge peptide domain (V253-S271), while the long hinge peptide (long-HP) encompassed both the hinge peptide domain and the adjacent C-terminal sequence (C272-R276) (Fig.\xa09c). We tested the binding affinity of these peptides to TfR1 under various pH conditions. The binding level of short-HP remained low and unchanged across different pH levels, similar to the negative control Flag peptide (supplementary Table\xa05). In contrast, the binding capacity of long-HP to TfR1 increased in a pH 5.5 buffer, mirroring the behavior of the full-length Gd-IgA1 protein. This finding highlights the critical role of the adjacent C-terminal sequence (C272-R276) in the interaction between IgA1 and TfR1, particularly under acidic conditions. In the kinetic affinity assay, the long-HP peptide exhibited a slow association and fast dissociation pattern (Fig.\xa09d). Its affinity index (KD\u2009=\u20095.158\u2009×\u200910-5\u2009M), although lower than that of the full-length Gd-IgA1 protein, is within the affinity range typical for many small molecules with their receptors. We further synthesized hinge peptide with N-Acetylgalactosamine (GalNAc) modifications (Supplementary Fig.\xa07). Although the overall binding affinity did not change, the binding mode shifted to a slow association and slow dissociation pattern. This suggests that galactose-deficient modification may promote a more stable interaction between IgA1 and TfR1(Fig.\xa09e). In summary, the R276 site, along with the galactose-deficient IgA1 hinge peptide, is essential for binding to TfR1 under acidic conditions. These insights deepen our understanding of the molecular interactions driving Gd-IgA1 deposition and suggest potential therapeutic targets for inhibiting the Gd-IgA1-TfR1 interaction to alleviate IgAN.\n'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
